"File Security and Access Rights - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/file-security-and-access-rights)\nciteturn19view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/file-security-and-access-rights\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 136\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # File security and access rights\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Because files are cite5†securable objects , access to them is regulated by the access-control model that governs access to all other securable objects in Windows. For a detailed explanation of this model, see cite6†Access Control .\nL33: ## Security descriptors\nL34: You can specify a cite7†security descriptor for a file or directory when you call the cite8†CreateFile , cite9†CreateDirectory , or cite10†CreateDirectoryEx function. If you specify `NULL` for the lpSecurityAttributes parameter, the file or directory gets a default security descriptor. The access control lists (ACL) in the default security descriptor for a file or directory are inherited from its parent directory.\nL35: Note that a default security descriptor is assigned only when a file or directory is newly created, and not when it is renamed or moved.\nL36: To retrieve the security descriptor of a file or directory object, call the cite11†GetNamedSecurityInfo or cite12†GetSecurityInfo function. To change the security descriptor of a file or directory object, call the cite13†SetNamedSecurityInfo or cite14†SetSecurityInfo function.\nL37: ## File access rights\nL38: \nL39: The valid access rights for files and directories include the DELETE, READ_CONTROL, WRITE_DAC, WRITE_OWNER, and SYNCHRONIZE cite15†standard access rights . The table in cite16†File Access Rights Constants lists the access rights that are specific to files and directories.\nL40: Although the SYNCHRONIZE access right is defined within the cite15†standard access rights list as the right to specify a file handle in one of the wait functions, when using asynchronous file I/O operations you should wait on the event handle contained in a properly configured cite17†OVERLAPPED structure rather than using the file handle with the SYNCHRONIZE access right for synchronization.\nL41: \nL42: The following are the cite18†generic access rights for files and directories:\nL43: Generic access right  | Mapped access rights  | Generic meaning\nL44: --- | --- | ---\nL45: FILE_GENERIC_EXECUTE  | FILE_EXECUTE\nL46: FILE_READ_ATTRIBUTES\nL47: STANDARD_RIGHTS_EXECUTE\nL48: SYNCHRONIZE  | Execute access\nL49: FILE_GENERIC_READ  | FILE_READ_ATTRIBUTES\nL50: FILE_READ_DATA\nL51: FILE_READ_EA\nL52: STANDARD_RIGHTS_READ\nL53: SYNCHRONIZE  | Read access\nL54: FILE_GENERIC_WRITE  | FILE_APPEND_DATA\nL55: FILE_WRITE_ATTRIBUTES\nL56: FILE_WRITE_DATA\nL57: FILE_WRITE_EA\nL58: STANDARD_RIGHTS_WRITE\nL59: SYNCHRONIZE  | Write access\nL60: Windows compares the requested access rights and the information in the thread's access token with the information in the file or directory object's security descriptor. If the comparison does not prohibit all of the requested access rights from being granted, a handle to the object is returned to the thread and the access rights are granted. For more information about this process, see cite19†Interaction between Threads and Securable Objects .\nL61: By default, authorization for access to a file or directory is controlled strictly by the ACLs in the security descriptor associated with that file or directory. In particular, the security descriptor of a parent directory is not used to control access to any child file or directory. The FILE_TRAVERSE cite20†access right can be enforced by removing the BYPASS_TRAVERSE_CHECKING cite21†privilege from users.\nL62: This is not recommended in the general case, as many programs do not correctly handle directory traversal errors. The primary use for the FILE_TRAVERSE access right on directories is to enable conformance to certain IEEE and ISO POSIX standards when interoperability with Unix systems is a requirement.\nL63: The Windows security model provides a way for a child directory to inherit, or to be prevented from inheriting, one or more of the ACEs in the parent directory's security descriptor. Each ACE contains information that determines how it can be inherited, and whether it will have an effect on the inheriting directory object. For example, some inherited ACEs control access to the inherited directory object, and these are called effective ACEs. All other ACEs are called inherit-only ACEs.\nL64: The Windows security model also enforces the automatic inheritance of ACEs to child objects according to the cite22†ACE inheritance rules . This automatic inheritance, along with the inheritance information in each ACE, determines how security restrictions are passed down the directory hierarchy.\nL65: Note that you cannot use an access-denied ACE to deny only GENERIC_READ or only GENERIC_WRITE access to a file. This is because for file objects, the generic mappings for both GENERIC_READ or GENERIC_WRITE include the SYNCHRONIZE access right. If an ACE denies GENERIC_WRITE access to a trustee, and the trustee requests GENERIC_READ access, the request will fail because the request implicitly includes SYNCHRONIZE access which is implicitly denied by the ACE, and vice versa.\nL66: Instead of using access-denied ACEs, use access-allowed ACEs to explicitly allow the permitted access rights.\nL67: Another means of managing access to storage objects is encryption. The implementation of file system encryption in Windows is the Encrypted File System, or EFS. EFS encrypts only files and not directories. The advantage of encryption is that it provides additional protection to files that is applied on the media and not through the file system and the standard Windows access control architecture. For more information on file encryption, see cite23†File Encryption .\nL68: ## Backup and restore access rights\nL69: In most cases, the ability to read and write the security settings of a file or directory object is restricted to kernel-mode processes. Clearly, you would not want any user process to be able to change the ownership or access restriction on your private file or directory. However, a backup application would not be able to complete its job of backing up your file if the access restrictions you have placed on your file or directory does not allow the application's user-mode process to read it.\nL70: Backup applications must be able to override the security settings of file and directory objects to ensure a complete backup. Similarly, if a backup application attempts to write a backup copy of your file over the disk-resident copy, and you explicitly deny write privileges to the backup application process, the restore operation cannot complete. In this case also, the backup application must be able to override the access control settings of your file.\nL71: The SE_BACKUP_NAME and SE_RESTORE_NAME access privileges were specifically created to provide this ability to backup applications. If these privileges have been granted and enabled in the access token of the backup application process, it can then call cite8†CreateFile to open your file or directory for backup, specifying the standard READ_CONTROL access right as the value of the dwDesiredAccess parameter.\nL72: However, to identify the calling process as a backup process, the call to CreateFile must include the FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS flag in the dwFlagsAndAttributes parameter. The full syntax of the function call is the following:\nL73: \nL74:     `HANDLE hFile = CreateFile( fileName,                   // lpFileName\nL75:                                READ_CONTROL,               // dwDesiredAccess\nL76:                                0,                          // dwShareMode\nL77:                                NULL,                       // lpSecurityAttributes\nL78:                                OPEN_EXISTING,              // dwCreationDisposition\nL79:                                FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS, // dwFlagsAndAttributes\nL80:                                NULL );                     // hTemplateFile\nL81:     `\nL82: This will allow the backup application process to open your file and override the standard security checking. To restore your file, the backup application would use the following cite8†CreateFile call syntax when opening your file to be written.\nL83: \nL84:     `HANDLE hFile = CreateFile( fileName,                   // lpFileName\nL85:                                WRITE_OWNER | WRITE_DAC,    // dwDesiredAccess\nL86:                                0,                          // dwShareMode\nL87:                                NULL,                       // lpSecurityAttributes\nL88:                                CREATE_ALWAYS,              // dwCreationDisposition\nL89:                                FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS, // dwFlagsAndAttributes\nL90:                                NULL );                     // hTemplateFile\nL91:     `\nL92: There are situations when a backup application must be able to change the access control settings of a file or directory. An example is when the access control settings of the disk-resident copy of a file or directory is different from the backup copy. This would happen if these settings were changed after the file or directory was backed up, or if it was corrupted.\nL93: The FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS flag specified in the call to cite8†CreateFile gives the backup application process permission to read the access-control settings of the file or directory. With this permission, the backup application process can then call cite24†GetKernelObjectSecurity and cite25†SetKernelObjectSecurity to read and then reset the access-control settings.\nL94: If a backup application must have access to the system-level cite26†access control settings , the ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY flag must be specified in the dwDesiredAccess parameter value passed to cite8†CreateFile .\nL95: \nL96: Backup applications call cite27†BackupRead to read the files and directories specified for the restore operation, and cite28†BackupWrite to write them.\nL97: ## Related content\nL98: \nL99: cite15†Standard access rights L100: \nL101: * * *\nL102: \nL103: ## Feedback\nL104: \nL105: Was this page helpful?\nL106: \nL107: Yes No No\nL108: \nL109: Need help with this topic?\nL110: \nL111: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL112: \nL113: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL114: \nL115: Suggest a fix?\nL116: \nL117: * * *\nL118: \nL119: ## Additional resources\nL120: \nL121: * * *\nL122: \nL123:   * Last updated on 2025-07-09\nL124: \nL125: ### In this article\nL126: \nL127: Was this page helpful?\nL128: \nL129: Need help with this topic?\nL130: \nL131: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL132: \nL133: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL134: \nL135: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWell-known SIDs - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/well-known-sids)\nciteturn19view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/well-known-sids\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 316\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # Well-known SIDs\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: ## In this article\nL30: \nL31: Well-known cite5†security identifiers (SIDs) identify generic groups and generic users. For example, there are well-known SIDs to identify the following groups and users:\nL32: \nL33:   * Everyone or World, which is a group that includes all users.\nL34:   * CREATOR_OWNER, which is used as a placeholder in an inheritable ACE. When the ACE is inherited, the system replaces the CREATOR_OWNER SID with the SID of the object's creator.\nL35:   * The Administrators group for the built-in domain on the local computer.\nL36: There are cite6†universal well-known SIDs , which are meaningful on all secure systems using this security model, including operating systems other than Windows. In addition, there are well-known SIDs that are meaningful only on Windows systems.\nL37: The Windows API defines a set of constants for well-known identifier authority and cite7†relative identifier (RID) values. You can use these constants to create well-known SIDs. The following example combines the SECURITY_WORLD_SID_AUTHORITY and SECURITY_WORLD_RID constants to show the universal well-known SID for the special group representing all users (Everyone or World):\nL38: \nL39: `S-1-1-0`\nL40: This example uses the string notation for SIDs in which S identifies the string as a SID, the first 1 is the revision level of the SID, and the remaining two digits are the SECURITY_WORLD_SID_AUTHORITY and SECURITY_WORLD_RID constants.\nL41: You can use the cite8†AllocateAndInitializeSid function to build a SID by combining an identifier authority value with up to eight subauthority values. For example, to determine whether the logged-on user is a member of a particular well-known group, call AllocateAndInitializeSid to build a SID for the well-known group and use the cite9†EqualSid function to compare that SID to the group SIDs in the user's cite10†access token . For an example, see cite11†Searching for a SID in an Access Token in C++ .\nL42: You must call the cite12†FreeSid function to free a SID allocated by AllocateAndInitializeSid.\nL43: The remainder of this section contains tables of well-known SIDs and tables of identifier authority and subauthority constants that you can use to build well-known SIDs.\nL44: \nL45: The following are some cite6†universal well-known SIDs .\nL46: Universal well-known SID  | Identifies\nL47: --- | ---\nL48: Null SID\nL49: String value: `S-1-0-0`  | A group with no members. This is often used when a SID value is not known.\nL50: World\nL51: String value: `S-1-1-0`  | A group that includes all users.\nL52: Local\nL53: String value: `S-1-2-0`  | Users who log on to cite13†terminals locally (physically) connected to the system.\nL54: Creator Owner ID\nL55: String value: `S-1-3-0`  | A security identifier to be replaced by the security identifier of the user who created a new object. This SID is used in inheritable ACEs.\nL56: Creator Group ID\nL57: String value: `S-1-3-1`  | A security identifier to be replaced by the primary-group SID of the user who created a new object. Use this SID in inheritable ACEs.\nL58: The following table lists the predefined identifier authority constants. The first four values are used with universal well-known SIDs; the last value is used with Windows well-known SIDs.\nL59: \nL60: Identifier authority  | Value  | String value\nL61: --- | --- | ---\nL62: SECURITY_NULL_SID_AUTHORITY  | `0`  | `S-1-0`\nL63: SECURITY_WORLD_SID_AUTHORITY  | `1`  | `S-1-1`\nL64: SECURITY_LOCAL_SID_AUTHORITY  | `2`  | `S-1-2`\nL65: SECURITY_CREATOR_SID_AUTHORITY  | `3`  | `S-1-3`\nL66: SECURITY_NT_AUTHORITY  | `5`  | `S-1-5`\nL67: The following cite7†RID values are used with cite6†universal well-known SIDs . The Identifier authority column shows the prefix of the identifier authority with which you can combine the RID to create a universal well-known SID.\nL68: Relative identifier authority  | Value  | String value\nL69: --- | --- | ---\nL70: SECURITY_NULL_RID  | `0`  | `S-1-0-0`\nL71: SECURITY_WORLD_RID  | `0`  | `S-1-1-0`\nL72: SECURITY_LOCAL_RID  | `0`  | `S-1-2-0`\nL73: SECURITY_LOCAL_LOGON_RID  | `1`  | `S-1-2-1`\nL74: SECURITY_CREATOR_OWNER_RID  | `0`  | `S-1-3-0`\nL75: SECURITY_CREATOR_GROUP_RID  | `1`  | `S-1-3-1`\nL76: The SECURITY_NT_AUTHORITY (S-1-5) predefined identifier authority produces SIDs that are not universal but are meaningful only on Windows installations. You can use the following RID values with SECURITY_NT_AUTHORITY to create well-known SIDs.\nL77: Constant  | Identifies\nL78: --- | ---\nL79: SECURITY_DIALUP_RID\nL80: String value: `S-1-5-1`  | Users who log on to cite13†terminals using a dial-up modem. This is a group identifier.\nL81: SECURITY_NETWORK_RID\nL82: String value: `S-1-5-2`  | Users who log on across a network. This is a group identifier added to the token of a process when it was logged on across a network. The corresponding logon type is LOGON32_LOGON_NETWORK.\nL83: SECURITY_BATCH_RID\nL84: String value: `S-1-5-3`  | Users who log on using a batch queue facility. This is a group identifier added to the token of a process when it was logged as a batch job. The corresponding logon type is LOGON32_LOGON_BATCH.\nL85: SECURITY_INTERACTIVE_RID\nL86: String value: `S-1-5-4`  | Users who log on for interactive operation. This is a group identifier added to the token of a process when it was logged on interactively. The corresponding logon type is LOGON32_LOGON_INTERACTIVE.\nL87: SECURITY_LOGON_IDS_RID\nL88: String value: `S-1-5-5-*X*-*Y*`  | A logon session. This is used to ensure that only cite14†processes in a given logon session can gain access to the window-station objects for that session. The X and Y values for these SIDs are different for each logon session. The value SECURITY_LOGON_IDS_RID_COUNT is the number of RIDs in this identifier (5-X-Y).\nL89: SECURITY_SERVICE_RID\nL90: String value: `S-1-5-6`  | Accounts authorized to log on as a service. This is a group identifier added to the token of a process when it was logged as a service. The corresponding logon type is LOGON32_LOGON_SERVICE.\nL91: SECURITY_ANONYMOUS_LOGON_RID\nL92: String value: `S-1-5-7`  | Anonymous logon, or null session logon.\nL93: SECURITY_PROXY_RID\nL94: String value: `S-1-5-8`  | Proxy.\nL95: SECURITY_ENTERPRISE_CONTROLLERS_RID\nL96: String value: `S-1-5-9`  | Enterprise controllers.\nL97: SECURITY_PRINCIPAL_SELF_RID\nL98: String value: `S-1-5-10`  | The PRINCIPAL_SELF security identifier can be used in the ACL of a user or group object. During an access check, the system replaces the SID with the SID of the object. The PRINCIPAL_SELF SID is useful for specifying an inheritable ACE that applies to the user or group object that inherits the ACE. It the only way of representing the SID of a created object in the default cite5†security descriptor of the schema.\nL99: SECURITY_AUTHENTICATED_USER_RID\nL100: String value: `S-1-5-11`  | The authenticated users.\nL101: SECURITY_RESTRICTED_CODE_RID\nL102: String value: `S-1-5-12`  | Restricted code.\nL103: SECURITY_TERMINAL_SERVER_RID\nL104: String value: `S-1-5-13`  | Terminal Services. Automatically added to the security token of a user who logs on to a terminal server.\nL105: SECURITY_LOCAL_SYSTEM_RID\nL106: String value: `S-1-5-18`  | A special account used by the operating system.\nL107: SECURITY_NT_NON_UNIQUE\nL108: String value: `S-1-5-21`  | SIDS are not unique.\nL109: SECURITY_BUILTIN_DOMAIN_RID\nL110: String value: `S-1-5-32`  | The built-in system domain.\nL111: SECURITY_WRITE_RESTRICTED_CODE_RID\nL112: String value: `S-1-5-33`  | Write restricted code.\nL113: SECURITY_RESTRICTED_SERVICES_BASE_RID\nL114: String value:`S-1-5-99`  | Restricted Services.\nL115: The following RIDs are relative to each domain.\nL116: RID  | Identifies\nL117: --- | ---\nL118: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_RAS_SERVERS\nL119: Value: `0x00000229`  | A local group that represents RAS and IAS servers. This group permits access to various attributes of user objects.\nL120: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_CERTSVC_DCOM_ACCESS_GROUP\nL121: Value: `0x0000023E`  | The group of users who can connect to certification authorities using Distributed Component Object Model (DCOM).\nL122: DOMAIN_USER_RID_ADMIN\nL123: Value: `0x000001F4`  | The administrative user account in a domain.\nL124: DOMAIN_USER_RID_GUEST\nL125: Value: `0x000001F5`  | The guest-user account in a domain. Users who do not have an account can automatically log on to this account.\nL126: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_ADMINS\nL127: Value: `0x00000200`  | The domain administrators' group. This account exists only on systems running server operating systems.\nL128: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_USERS\nL129: Value: `0x00000201`  | A group that contains all user accounts in a domain. All users are automatically added to this group.\nL130: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_GUESTS\nL131: Value: `0x00000202`  | The guest-group account in a domain.\nL132: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_COMPUTERS\nL133: Value: `0x00000203`  | The domain computers' group. All computers in the domain are members of this group.\nL134: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_CONTROLLERS\nL135: Value: `0x00000204`  | The domain controllers' group. All DCs in the domain are members of this group.\nL136: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_CERT_ADMINS\nL137: Value: `0x00000205`  | The certificate publishers' group. Computers running Certificate Services are members of this group.\nL138: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_ENTERPRISE_READONLY_DOMAIN_CONTROLLERS\nL139: Value: `0x000001F2`  | The group of enterprise read-only domain controllers.\nL140: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_SCHEMA_ADMINS\nL141: Value: `0x00000206`  | The schema administrators' group. Members of this group can modify the Active Directory schema.\nL142: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_ENTERPRISE_ADMINS\nL143: Value: `0x00000207`  | The enterprise administrators' group. Members of this group have full access to all domains in the Active Directory forest. Enterprise administrators are responsible for forest-level operations such as adding or removing new domains.\nL144: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_POLICY_ADMINS\nL145: Value: `0x00000208`  | The policy administrators' group.\nL146: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_READONLY_CONTROLLERS\nL147: Value: `0x00000209`  | The group of read-only domain controllers\nL148: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_CLONEABLE_CONTROLLERS\nL149: Value: `0x0000020A`  | The group of cloneable domain controllers.\nL150: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_CDC_RESERVED\nL151: Value: `0x0000020C`  | The reserved CDC group.\nL152: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_PROTECTED_USERS\nL153: Value: `0x0000020D`  | The protected users group.\nL154: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_KEY_ADMINS\nL155: Value: `0x0000020E`  | The key admins group.\nL156: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_ENTERPRISE_KEY_ADMINS\nL157: Value: `0x0000020F`  | The enterprise key admins group.\nL158: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_FOREST_TRUSTS\nL159: Value: `0x00000210`  | The primary group of forest trust user accounts.\nL160: DOMAIN_GROUP_RID_EXTERNAL_TRUSTS\nL161: Value: `0x00000211`  | The primary group of external trust user accounts.\nL162: The following RIDs are used to specify mandatory integrity level.\nL163: RID  | Value  | Identifies\nL164: --- | --- | ---\nL165: SECURITY_MANDATORY_UNTRUSTED_RID  | `0x00000000`  | Untrusted.\nL166: SECURITY_MANDATORY_LOW_RID  | `0x00001000`  | Low integrity.\nL167: SECURITY_MANDATORY_MEDIUM_RID  | `0x00002000`  | Medium integrity.\nL168: SECURITY_MANDATORY_MEDIUM_PLUS_RID  | SECURITY_MANDATORY_MEDIUM_RID + `0x100`  | Medium high integrity.\nL169: SECURITY_MANDATORY_HIGH_RID  | `0X00003000`  | High integrity.\nL170: SECURITY_MANDATORY_SYSTEM_RID  | `0x00004000`  | System integrity.\nL171: SECURITY_MANDATORY_PROTECTED_PROCESS_RID  | `0x00005000`  | Protected process.\nL172: The following table has examples of domain-relative RIDs that you can use to form well-known SIDs for local groups (aliases). For more information about local and global groups, see cite15†Local Group Functions and cite16†Group Functions .\nL173: RID  | Identifies\nL174: --- | ---\nL175: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_ADMINS\nL176: Value: `0x00000220`\nL177: String value: `S-1-5-32-544`  | A local group used for administration of the domain.\nL178: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_USERS\nL179: Value: `0x00000221`\nL180: String value: `S-1-5-32-545`  | A local group that represents all users in the domain.\nL181: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_GUESTS\nL182: Value: `0x00000222`\nL183: String value: `S-1-5-32-546`  | A local group that represents guests of the domain.\nL184: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_POWER_USERS\nL185: Value: `0x00000223`\nL186: String value: `S-1-5-32-547`  | A local group used to represent a user or set of users who expect to treat a system as if it were their personal computer rather than as a workstation for multiple users.\nL187: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_ACCOUNT_OPS\nL188: Value: `0x00000224`\nL189: String value: `S-1-5-32-548`  | A local group that exists only on systems running server operating systems. This local group permits control over nonadministrator accounts.\nL190: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_SYSTEM_OPS\nL191: Value: `0x00000225`\nL192: String value: `S-1-5-32-549`  | A local group that exists only on systems running server operating systems. This local group performs system administrative functions, not including security functions. It establishes network shares, controls printers, unlocks workstations, and performs other operations.\nL193: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_PRINT_OPS\nL194: Value: `0x00000226`\nL195: String value: `S-1-5-32-550`  | A local group that exists only on systems running server operating systems. This local group controls printers and print queues.\nL196: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_BACKUP_OPS\nL197: Value: `0x00000227`\nL198: String value: `S-1-5-32-551`  | A local group used for controlling assignment of file backup-and-restore privileges.\nL199: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_REPLICATOR\nL200: Value: `0x00000228`\nL201: String value: `S-1-5-32-552`  | A local group responsible for copying security databases from the primary domain controller to the backup domain controllers. These accounts are used only by the system.\nL202: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_PREW2KCOMPACCESS\nL203: Value: `0x0000022A`\nL204: String value: `S-1-5-32-554`  | A local group that exists only on systems running Windows 2000 Server. For more information, see cite17†Allowing Anonymous Access .\nL205: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_REMOTE_DESKTOP_USERS\nL206: Value: `0x0000022B`\nL207: String value: `S-1-5-32-555`  | A local group that represents all remote desktop users.\nL208: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_NETWORK_CONFIGURATION_OPS\nL209: Value: `0x0000022C`\nL210: String value: `S-1-5-32-556`  | A local group that represents the network configuration.\nL211: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_INCOMING_FOREST_TRUST_BUILDERS\nL212: Value: `0x0000022D`\nL213: String value: `S-1-5-32-557`  | A local group that represents any forest trust users.\nL214: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_MONITORING_USERS\nL215: Value: `0x0000022E`\nL216: String value: `S-1-5-32-558`  | A local group that represents all users being monitored.\nL217: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_LOGGING_USERS\nL218: Value: `0x0000022F`\nL219: String value: `S-1-5-32-559`  | A local group responsible for logging users.\nL220: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_AUTHORIZATIONACCESS\nL221: Value: `0x00000230`\nL222: String value: `S-1-5-32-560`  | A local group that represents all authorized access.\nL223: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_TS_LICENSE_SERVERS\nL224: Value: `0x00000231`\nL225: String value: `S-1-5-32-561`  | A local group that exists only on systems running server operating systems that allow for terminal services and remote access.\nL226: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_DCOM_USERS\nL227: Value: `0x00000232`\nL228: String value: `S-1-5-32-562`  | A local group that represents users who can use Distributed Component Object Model (DCOM).\nL229: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_IUSERS\nL230: Value: `0X00000238`\nL231: String value: `S-1-5-32-568`  | A local group that represents Internet users.\nL232: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_CRYPTO_OPERATORS\nL233: Value: `0x00000239`\nL234: String value: `S-1-5-32-569`  | A local group that represents access to cryptography operators.\nL235: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_CACHEABLE_PRINCIPALS_GROUP\nL236: Value: `0x0000023B`\nL237: String value: `S-1-5-32-571`  | A local group that represents principals that can be cached.\nL238: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_NON_CACHEABLE_PRINCIPALS_GROUP\nL239: Value: `0x0000023C`\nL240: String value: `S-1-5-32-572`  | A local group that represents principals that cannot be cached.\nL241: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_EVENT_LOG_READERS_GROUP\nL242: Value: `0x0000023D`\nL243: String value: `S-1-5-32-573`  | A local group that represents event log readers.\nL244: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_CERTSVC_DCOM_ACCESS_GROUP\nL245: Value: `0x0000023E`\nL246: String value: `S-1-5-32-574`  | The local group of users who can connect to certification authorities using Distributed Component Object Model (DCOM).\nL247: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_RDS_REMOTE_ACCESS_SERVERS\nL248: Value: `0x0000023F`\nL249: String value: `S-1-5-32-575`  | A local group that represents RDS remote access servers.\nL250: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_RDS_ENDPOINT_SERVERS\nL251: Value: `0x00000240`\nL252: String value: `S-1-5-32-576`  | A local group that represents endpoint servers.\nL253: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_RDS_MANAGEMENT_SERVERS\nL254: Value: `0x00000241`\nL255: String value: `S-1-5-32-577`  | A local group that represents management servers.\nL256: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_HYPER_V_ADMINS\nL257: Value: `0x00000242`\nL258: String value: `S-1-5-32-578`  | A local group that represents hyper-v admins.\nL259: DOMAIN_ALIAS_RID_ACCESS_CONTROL_ASSISTANCE_OPS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGetApplicationRestartSettings function (winbase.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-getapplicationrestartsettings)\nciteturn19view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-getapplicationrestartsettings\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 207\n"
